A||||6||montage||Fusable link-ignition?||||1||
Z||000000||montage||01-15-2001||10:27 AM||||On my way to work in my 88 formula this morning and the voltage gauge, radio, and tach all started oscillating. The voltage was down around 8-10 V. The car died as I tried to make it back home. The electrical is completely dead when you turn the ignition  (lights still work). There appears to be a fusible link coming from the battery terminal block next to the battery that is fried. Anyone had this problem before or has any comments on what could have caused this to fry. I have a digital pic on my computer but since I'm new here can't figure out how to post without putting it on a web page (I can post on a web page at work). Thanks in advance for you help (awesome forum)!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||01-15-2001||12:01 PM||||I had that one go on my '88 one morning during rush hour! (back in '91)<BR>I was fortunate enough to have some wire with me to make a temporary jumper and made it to work.<BR>I replaced the fuse lnik and havn't had any problems with it ever again! I contribute it with a dirty connection as I found no other cause.<BR>Replace it and see if it duplicates! Clean the terminal connections at the block as it not well protected and gets weather!||||reg||1
Z||000002||sqoach||01-15-2001||12:23 PM||||happened to me a few months ago. just driving down the road and my car turned off. and the lights did still work. i'm not exactly sure what caused it, probably just getting old. i used a regular wire to get it home. but it melted the plastic coating around it. i used electrical tape after that and it's been ok ever since.<P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig_2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||William Federle||01-15-2001||01:10 PM||||This happened to me last summer. It was caused by the radiator fan seizing up (my fault due to a poor repair job). My fusible link (actually 2 of them) was connected to the big terminal on the starter solenoid. <P>I moved both fusible links to where it sounds like yours are now - up into the battery compartment. It's much more road-repairable now.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 01-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||01-16-2001||09:01 PM||||Fuse links can crap out just from age.<P>When fuse links are replaced they must be crimped. Usually a crimp is provided with the link. At least on all the ones I've seen it was.<P>Pack the crimps with RTV silicone to keep water out.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||01-16-2001||10:39 PM||||I used to "play" with boats and found that if you go to a marine store you can get adhesive lined crimp connectors. You crimp them on then heat the shrink tube ends and you have a 100% water proof connection. Works great even down in the bilge which usually has a bunch of salt water in it.||||reg||1
Z||000006||montage||01-17-2001||08:28 AM||||I soldered a 16 ga wire in place of the fusible link (so I could go get a fusible link) and by the time I got to the parts store the wire started frying (12 mi). I put the new fusible link on and so far it's working ok. Wonder why the hook up wire fried the gauge was right maybe poor insulation? Just curious if anyone has commnets. Again thanks for the help!||||reg||1
